---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../consts';
// Working title. Final product name is Deven's call (see plan doc: naming is an
// open decision). Slug is /aeo-readiness-check; /readiness-check 301s to it (public/_redirects).
---
<BaseLayout
  title="AEO Readiness Check | Can AI Actually Read Your Site?"
  description="Free single-URL scan. See whether the crawlers behind ChatGPT, Perplexity, Claude, and Gemini can read, parse, and cite your site."
>
  <main>
    <!-- Hero -->
    <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 pt-20 pb-12 md:pt-28">
      <p class="font-mono text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-impact">// run the check</p>
      <h1 class="mt-5 max-w-4xl text-5xl md:text-6xl">Can AI actually read your site?</h1>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-2xl text-lg text-canvas/80">
        Most B2B SaaS sites have never been checked against the crawlers that
        decide AI citations. This scan checks yours in seconds, against the
        access rules those crawlers actually follow.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- The form -->
    <section class="border-t border-white/10">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-12">
        <form id="scan-form" method="post" action="/api/scan" class="max-w-2xl" novalidate>
          <label class="block">
            <span class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-canvas/50">Your site *</span>
            <input name="url" type="text" required maxlength="2000" autocomplete="url" inputmode="url"
              class="mt-2 w-full border border-white/20 bg-white/[0.03] px-4 py-3 font-mono text-canvas placeholder:text-canvas/30 focus:border-impact focus:outline-none"
              placeholder="yourcompany.com" />
          </label>

          <!-- Turnstile widget: verified server-side; this is the bot gate. -->
          <div class="cf-turnstile mt-5" data-sitekey={SITE.turnstileSiteKey} data-theme="dark"></div>

          <div class="mt-5 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-5">
            <button type="submit"
              class="bg-impact px-8 py-4 font-display text-lg font-bold text-canvas transition-opacity hover:opacity-90 disabled:opacity-40">
              Run the check
            </button>
            <p id="scan-status" role="status" aria-live="polite" class="font-mono text-sm text-canvas/60"></p>
          </div>

          <!-- Email is not collected here. Results show score-only until the
               visitor chooses to unlock full findings below (two-step reveal,
               matches the hybrid model: value first, then the ask). -->
        </form>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Results: hidden until a scan completes; populated by /js/readiness-check.js -->
    <section id="results" class="hidden border-t border-white/10" aria-live="polite">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-16">
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-canvas/50">// machine readout</p>
        <div id="results-body" class="mt-6"></div>

        <!-- Unlock box: shown only when the API returns a gated result
             (no valid email yet). Hidden entirely once unlocked, since at
             that point results-body already has the full findings. -->
        <div id="unlock-box" class="hidden mt-10 border border-impact/40 bg-white/[0.02] p-8 max-w-2xl">
          <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-impact">// see the full breakdown</p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-canvas/80">
            The score is free. What to fix, and which crawlers are actually
            blocked, is behind your email.
          </p>
          <div class="mt-5 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4">
            <label class="sr-only" for="unlock-email">Email</label>
            <input id="unlock-email" type="email" required autocomplete="email" placeholder="you@company.com"
              class="min-w-0 flex-1 border border-white/20 bg-white/[0.03] px-4 py-3 font-mono text-canvas placeholder:text-canvas/30 focus:border-impact focus:outline-none" />
            <button id="unlock-submit" type="button"
              class="bg-impact px-6 py-3 font-display font-bold text-canvas transition-opacity hover:opacity-90 disabled:opacity-40 whitespace-nowrap">
              Unlock full report
            </button>
          </div>
          <p id="unlock-status" role="status" aria-live="polite" class="mt-3 font-mono text-sm text-canvas/60"></p>
        </div>
        <div class="mt-12 border border-white/15 bg-white/[0.02] p-8 max-w-2xl">
          <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-canvas/50">// what now</p>
          <h2 class="mt-3 text-2xl">A score tells you where you stand. It doesn&rsquo;t fix anything.</h2>
          <p class="mt-3 text-canvas/70">
            The full audit goes where a scan can&rsquo;t: your entity graph, your
            citation record, and what the gap is costing you. That&rsquo;s the work.
          </p>
          <a href={SITE.booking} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="mt-6 inline-block border border-white/30 px-6 py-3 font-display font-bold text-canvas transition-colors hover:border-impact">
            Book a call
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Bottom line: plain-English summary for first-time visitors. -->
    <section class="border-t border-white/10">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-16">
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-canvas/50">// the bottom line</p>
        <h2 class="mt-3 max-w-3xl text-3xl">This scores whether AI systems can read your site. Not Google.</h2>
        <p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl text-lg text-canvas/80">
          The check runs three tests: crawl access, structured data, and page
          speed. It rolls them into one grade, A through F. The weighting is
          deliberate. Being reachable by the right crawlers counts for almost
          half the grade, because nothing else on the list matters if a crawler
          never gets in the door.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Three things it measures -->
    <section class="border-t border-white/10">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-16">
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-canvas/50">// what this measures</p>
        <h2 class="mt-3 max-w-3xl text-3xl">Three things decide whether AI can cite you. This checks all three.</h2>
        <div class="mt-10 grid gap-8 md:grid-cols-3">
          <div class="border-t-2 border-infra pt-5">
            <h3 class="text-xl">Crawl access</h3>
            <p class="mt-3 text-canvas/70">
              Whether the crawlers behind ChatGPT, Perplexity, Claude, and Gemini
              are allowed to read your site at all. If a crawler is blocked,
              nothing else here matters. It never gets far enough to notice.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="border-t-2 border-ai pt-5">
            <h3 class="text-xl">Structured data</h3>
            <p class="mt-3 text-canvas/70">
              Whether your pages carry valid schema.org markup describing what
              your company is. Machines lean on structure over prose. No
              structured data means they are guessing.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="border-t-2 border-success pt-5">
            <h3 class="text-xl">Delivery speed</h3>
            <p class="mt-3 text-canvas/70">
              How fast your page loads, with extra weight on how fast the server
              answers first. AI crawlers give up in seconds and mostly do not run
              JavaScript. A slow page may as well be empty.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- How the overall score is calculated -->
    <section class="border-t border-white/10">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-16">
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-canvas/50">// how the score works</p>
        <h2 class="mt-3 max-w-3xl text-3xl">How the overall score is calculated.</h2>
        <p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl text-canvas/80">
          The three scores do not average evenly. Crawl access counts for almost
          half the grade, and a total crawl block overrides everything else.
        </p>
        <dl class="mt-10 grid gap-8 md:grid-cols-3">
          <div class="border-t-2 border-infra pt-5">
            <dt class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-canvas/50">Crawl access</dt>
            <dd class="mt-2 font-mono text-5xl font-bold text-canvas">45%</dd>
          </div>
          <div class="border-t-2 border-ai pt-5">
            <dt class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-canvas/50">Structured data</dt>
            <dd class="mt-2 font-mono text-5xl font-bold text-canvas">30%</dd>
          </div>
          <div class="border-t-2 border-success pt-5">
            <dt class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-canvas/50">Delivery speed</dt>
            <dd class="mt-2 font-mono text-5xl font-bold text-canvas">25%</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
        <p class="mt-10 max-w-3xl text-canvas/70">
          If a site blocks every crawler outright, the overall grade is capped at
          the crawl score itself. No amount of speed or structured data can pull
          it back up. A blocked crawler never gets far enough to benefit from
          either.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-canvas/70">
          If a test cannot be measured on a given run, it is left out and the
          remaining weights rebalance. A measurement failure is never scored as a
          bad site.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Citation vs training crawlers -->
    <section class="border-t border-white/10">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-16">
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-canvas/50">// crawler types</p>
        <h2 class="mt-3 max-w-3xl text-3xl">Not every AI crawler matters the same way.</h2>
        <p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl text-canvas/80">
          A generic scanner reports &ldquo;GPTBot: blocked&rdquo; as if that is
          automatically a problem. It is not. Blocking a training crawler is a
          legitimate business choice. This check separates the two.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-10 grid gap-8 md:grid-cols-2">
          <div class="border-t-2 border-ai pt-5">
            <h3 class="text-xl">Citation crawlers</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-sm text-ai">OAI-SearchBot, PerplexityBot, Claude-User, Google-Extended</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-canvas/70">
              These drive real AI answer-engine visibility. Blocking them is
              usually accidental self-harm.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="border-t-2 border-infra pt-5">
            <h3 class="text-xl">Training crawlers</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-sm text-infra">GPTBot, ClaudeBot, CCBot</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-canvas/70">
              These feed model training. Blocking them is common and does not
              hurt citation visibility.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-10 max-w-3xl text-canvas/70">
          The check only flags a real problem when a citation crawler is blocked.
          Those are the ones that decide whether ChatGPT, Perplexity, or Gemini
          cite your site.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-10 max-w-2xl text-sm text-canvas/60">
          This scan reports what is publicly fetchable. Some sites serve
          different responses to different crawlers; where we can&rsquo;t read a
          file, the readout says so instead of guessing.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <!-- Turnstile loader (external, from Cloudflare, allowed in CSP) -->
  <script is:inline src="https://challenges.cloudflare.com/turnstile/v0/api.js" async defer></script>

  <script is:inline src="/js/readiness-check.js" defer></script>
</BaseLayout>
